        new("r-b1-4", "B1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Why Teenagers Need More Sleep",
            "Many parents complain that their teenage children stay up late and are impossible to wake up in the morning. It is easy to call this laziness, but scientists say the real reason is biology.\n\nDuring the teenage years, the body's internal clock changes. The brain starts to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, about two hours later than it does in children or adults. As a result, a fifteen-year-old may simply not feel tired until eleven o'clock or midnight. However, teenagers still need between eight and ten hours of sleep every night to stay healthy.\n\nWhen school starts early, many teenagers get only six or seven hours. Research shows that this lack of sleep can affect memory, mood and concentration. Tired students also find it harder to control their emotions and are more likely to get ill.\n\nSome schools have tried starting lessons later, at nine instead of eight. In several of these schools, attendance improved, and students said they felt more awake in class. Experts also give simple advice to families: keep phones out of the bedroom at night, and try to go to bed at the same time every day, even at weekends.",
            [
